30	    /// ### What it does
31	    ///
32	    /// Disallows variable declarations, imports, or type declarations that are
33	    /// not used in code.
34	    ///
35	    /// ### Why is this bad?
36	    ///
37	    /// Variables that are declared and not used anywhere in the code are most
38	    /// likely an error due to incomplete refactoring. Such variables take up
39	    /// space in the code and can lead to confusion by readers.
40	    ///
41	    /// ```ts
42	    /// // `b` is unused; this indicates a bug.
43	    /// function add(a: number, b: number) {
44	    ///     return a;
45	    /// }
46	    /// console.log(add(1, 2));
47	    /// ```
48	    ///
49	    /// A variable `foo` is considered to be used if any of the following are
50	    /// true:
51	    ///
52	    /// * It is called (`foo()`) or constructed (`new foo()`)
53	    /// * It is read (`var bar = foo`)
54	    /// * It is passed into a function or constructor as an argument (`doSomething(foo)`)
55	    /// * It is read inside of a function that is passed to another function
56	    ///   (`doSomething(function() { foo(); })`)
57	    /// * It is exported (`export const foo = 42`)
58	    /// * It is used as an operand to TypeScript's `typeof` operator (`const bar:
59	    ///   typeof foo = 4`)
60	    ///
61	    /// A variable is _not_ considered to be used if it is only ever declared
62	    /// (`var foo = 5`) or assigned to (`foo = 7`).
63	    ///
64	    /// #### Types
65	    /// This rule has full support for TypeScript types, interfaces, enums, and
66	    /// namespaces.
67	    ///
68	    /// A type or interface `Foo` is considered to be used if it is used in any
69	    /// of the following ways:
70	    /// - It is used in the definition of another type or interface.
71	    /// - It is used as a type annotation or as part of a function signature.
72	    /// - It is used in a cast or `satisfies` expression.
73	    ///
74	    /// A type or interface is _not_ considered to be used if it is only ever
75	    /// used in its own definition, e.g. `type Foo = Array<Foo>`.
76	    ///
77	    /// Enums and namespaces are treated the same as variables, classes,
78	    /// functions, etc.
79	    ///
80	    /// #### Ignored Files
81	    /// This rule ignores `.d.ts`, `.astro`, `.svelte` and `.vue` files entirely. Variables,
82	    /// classes, interfaces, and types declared in `.d.ts` files are generally
83	    /// used by other files, which are not checked by Oxlint. Since Oxlint does
84	    /// not support parsing template syntax, this rule cannot tell if a variable
85	    /// is used or unused in a Vue / Svelte / Astro file.
86	    ///
87	    /// #### Exported
88	    ///
89	    /// The original ESLint rule recognizes `/* exported variableName */`
90	    /// comments as a way to indicate that a variable is used in another script
91	    /// and should not be considered unused. Since ES modules are now a TC39
92	    /// standard, Oxlint does not support this feature.
93	    ///
94	    /// ### Examples
95	    ///
96	    /// Examples of **incorrect** code for this rule:
97	    ///
98	    /// ```javascript
99	    /// /* no-unused-vars: "error" */
100	    /// /* if you have `some_unused_var` defined as a global in .oxlintrc.json */
101	    ///
102	    /// // It checks variables you have defined as global
103	    /// some_unused_var = 42;
104	    ///
105	    /// var x;
106	    ///
107	    /// // Write-only variables are not considered as used.
108	    /// var y = 10;
109	    /// y = 5;
110	    ///
111	    /// // A read for a modification of itself is not considered as used.
112	    /// var z = 0;
113	    /// z = z + 1;
114	    ///
115	    /// // By default, unused arguments cause warnings.
116	    /// (function(foo) {
117	    ///     return 5;
118	    /// })();
119	    ///
120	    /// // Unused recursive functions also cause warnings.
121	    /// function fact(n) {
122	    ///     if (n < 2) return 1;
123	    ///     return n * fact(n - 1);
124	    /// }
125	    ///
126	    /// // When a function definition destructures an array, unused entries from
